WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:27.360
KENNY NING: So before
we get started,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.400
let's introduce ourselves.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.110
My name is Kenny.

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:33.560
I've been working at Spotify
for about a year and a half now.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.280
I'm on the Analytics
Departments,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.876
and I work on this team
called Analytics Consulting.

00:00:38.876 --> 00:00:40.500
Essentially, what
that means is we have

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.732
two departments at Spotify.

00:00:41.732 --> 00:00:43.940
There's an analytics team
and there's a content team.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.148
The content team is responsible
for getting the music

00:00:46.148 --> 00:00:47.900
on the service, et cetera.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.540
And I function as the interface
between the analytics team

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.832
and that content
part of the business.

00:00:53.832 --> 00:00:55.040
EMILY SAMUELS: And I'm Emily.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.070
I'm a Data Engineer
at Spotify, and I

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.070
work on music recommendations.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.560
My team does things
like discover

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.982
weekly, related artists, and
playlist recommendations.

00:01:04.982 --> 00:01:05.690
KENNY NING: Cool.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:06.190
All right.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.992
So before we get started
talking about BigQuery,

00:01:09.992 --> 00:01:11.700
maybe it makes sense
to give some context

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.470
around where we've come actually
from a data infrastructure

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.870
perspective at Spotify.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.920
So ever since the
beginning of Spotify,

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.817
we've always managed our own
on-premise Hadoop cluster.

00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:28.150
This is where it's great for
us over the past few years.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.710
And even most recently, we're
kind of famous in that we're

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:33.260
the largest.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.840
We have the largest
Hadoop cluster in Europe.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.120
We have about 2,300 nodes.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.010
And we run about
11,000 jobs a day.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.420
So a lot of work
being done there.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.600
And historically, this
is kind of the model

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.645
with which we've been able to
get data out of our cluster.

00:01:49.645 --> 00:01:52.020
More recently, we've been
experimenting with technologies

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.080
like Scalding and Crunch for
our [INAUDIBLE] production jobs.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.579
But in the past, we still have
a lot of jobs actually still

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:01.870
in production that are still
functioning off of the old

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.550
out-of-the-box Python MapReduce.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:09.050
That's for stuff that's
scheduled most of the time.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.990
If we want to do any kind of
ad hoc analysis on our data,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.490
we typically-- our
analysts will use

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:18.380
Hive, which is a SQL
language essentially,

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:24.290
for the more one-off ad hoc
kind of analysis pieces.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.864
So yeah, we were doing
this for a while.

00:02:27.864 --> 00:02:29.780
But the problem with
this model is it really--

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.280
there's two main
problems, actually,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.347
that were becoming a little
bit more than just problems.

00:02:34.347 --> 00:02:36.930
So the first thing, obviously
as you know, all of these things

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.390
are MapReduce.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.620
And if you haven't
used MapReduce before,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.540
it scales great.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.760
But the speed of iteration is
actually just incredibly slow.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.810
You're writing a query that
will take you a few minutes.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.970
Sometimes, hours, depending
on how complicated it is.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.030
And this speed was just
becoming a huge problem.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.900
Specifically, I'm
thinking maybe it's

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.730
difficult-- it becomes difficult
to report on time-sensitive

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:02.230
asks.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.240
And when I say "time-sensitive,"
imagine a scenario where Taylor

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.097
Swift-- you get an email from
your boss and it tells you that

00:03:10.097 --> 00:03:11.930
Taylor Swift's taking
her music off Spotify.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.400
You're like, OK,
this is terrible.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.260
I know.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:20.064
But you need to come back
with metrics within the hour

00:03:20.064 --> 00:03:22.230
telling your boss, like how
many playlists she's on.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.430
[? You have to get to PR ?]
quick, right?

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.530
But we can't actually do this
in a really reasonable way

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.400
to report on these
mission-critical pieces

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.250
because the query
takes like an hour.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.770
So sure, you could
argue that this is just

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.090
a one-off kind of thing.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.990
But as Spotify becomes more
and more in the public eye,

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.350
it's becoming more
and more intolerable

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.960
if we're slow to report on
these very mission-critical

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.462
time-sensitive pieces.

00:03:48.462 --> 00:03:49.920
So that was becoming
a big problem.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.830
Next.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:59.252
So analytics, we don't just
fight fires all the time.

00:03:59.252 --> 00:04:01.460
And I don't think analytics
organizations necessarily

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.170
want to do that and spend
their time doing that.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.940
Ideally, you want to
evolve as an organization

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:12.140
to spend most of your time doing
complex, less obvious answers,

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.810
and doing kind of
research things.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.900
However, the problem with us--
the main blocker for analytics

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.280
to actually evolve into
this good data science

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.977
organization, the
main blocker there

00:04:24.977 --> 00:04:26.560
was just the speed
of our technologies

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:27.800
that we were using.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.855
You can imagine, sure,
we gave this Taylor Swift

00:04:29.855 --> 00:04:33.760
a playlist number, but now your
stakeholder wants to come back

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.000
and say, I want to understand
something about the users.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.140
Can we look at the
listeners of Taylor Swift

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.300
before the take-down?

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.940
Can we look at them after?

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.350
Can we come up with some
story that something bad

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.870
happened with those users?

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.450
So this analysis,
as you know, there's

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.267
tons of things you can look at.

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:54.100
And it's really unclear
exactly what numbers

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.709
you want to pull at first.

00:04:55.709 --> 00:04:57.250
Ideally, you want
an environment that

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.560
allows you to iterate quickly
and try a bunch of-- test

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:01.980
a bunch of your
early hypotheses.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.250
Maybe we look at
their churn rates.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.010
Maybe we look at how they're
engaging with other replacement

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.150
artists.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.377
You want to test
all of these things.

00:05:10.377 --> 00:05:13.840
The problem is you're
using this technology where

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.870
every single query costs
you an hour of your time.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.829
And you have to really
think carefully.

00:05:18.829 --> 00:05:20.370
And you really don't
have the freedom

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.330
to explore as much
as you'd like to.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.200
And that just really
hampers your ability

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.650
to do these multi-step
exploratory analyses.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.905
So those were suffering a
lot also from the technology.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.820
Lastly, I'm sure
everyone whose analysis

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.510
dealt with this
before, but we'd run

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.990
into a really frustrating
situation where you'd kick off

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.220
something that was running for
several minutes, several hours,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.340
et cetera.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.850
95% of the way
complete and it just

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:55.900
fails at some single point
in the step of the job.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.050
Hitting a data set that
doesn't exist anymore

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.890
or some weird schema
changed or something.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:02.810
So those, in addition
to just being

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.590
common and really
frustrating to an analyst,

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:09.850
they also cost the system a lot
of resources that we couldn't

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.180
get back a lot of times.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.764
And this was a
headache for-- we're

00:06:13.764 --> 00:06:16.180
owning everything on-premise
and these data infrastructure

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.475
engineers have to debug where
these failures are happening.

00:06:19.475 --> 00:06:22.100
Especially, the case may be you
have this really bad job that's

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:23.424
just cronned.

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:25.340
You're not really focusing
on it and it's just

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.590
taking all these resources
that you can never get back.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.870
And sure, there existed tooling.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:31.990
We could write unitests.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.540
We could do things to help
prevent these problems,

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:38.610
but it wasn't at a state
where it was entirely usable.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.810
And you know, these
failures kept happening.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.820
So that's on the speed side.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:47.980
The other main piece
that was a huge problem

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.750
with using this on-premise
Hadoop cluster was we

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.500
needed to maintain a significant
amount of tooling to actually

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:54.840
make it usable.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:59.940
So especially not
everyone doing analysis

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.190
has a computer science degree
or can write a Scalding job.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.470
Most of them, their skill set
is more in line with statistics

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:11.220
and they know SQL at
best, a lot of cases.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.980
So you can't really
expect an analyst

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.420
to be able to SSH
into an edge node

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.400
and actually write
this job, kick it off.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.320
So we have to build things
like internal web UIs that

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.430
would allow analysts to
just go to a website,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.620
write their own SQL
query, execute something.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.200
But then, this is a product.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.700
And now we're in
a situation where

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.120
in addition to all the normal
data infrastructure things

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.370
we have to manage, we're
actually creating products now

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:44.160
for internal use that have
all the problems and overhead

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.390
and resource constraints
that come with a product.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:49.570
We have these web UIs
that we have to maintain.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.760
And we have to continue
logging and monitoring

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.180
the usage of this, as well
as the machines that we're

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.560
provisioning.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.240
There's other harder problems.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.810
If you write a
query in this web UI

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.710
and you want to get
millions of rows back,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:08.040
how do we actually send
that back to the end user?

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:12.660
So all this stuff is
largely, you could imagine,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.150
a fixed responsibility
if we want

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:16.830
to use this on-premise cluster.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.950
And we're investing really
talented, hardworking

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.910
engineers, maintaining
all this stuff

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:26.090
that maybe we don't
actually need.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:30.950
So we need a platform, really,
to solve these problems.

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:34.120
And BigQuery very
early on actually

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:39.309
jumped out as a really
great solution, particularly

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:42.179
number one, to address these two
problems of speed and tooling.

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:45.102
One, it was just
incredibly fast.

00:08:45.102 --> 00:08:46.560
I remember still,
actually, there's

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.980
like a really small
group of analysts

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:52.810
who were given first access at
Spotify to give the tool a try

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.920
and it was just like awesome.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:58.150
Being able to see getting
used to querying this one data

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:01.660
set that would take you--
being used to waiting 15,

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:03.314
20 minutes for this to run.

00:09:03.314 --> 00:09:04.730
And then, suddenly
getting results

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.300
back through BigQuery
within 10 seconds.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.190
That was just amazing to
me and super-inspiring.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:13.330
And really, got me excited
about all the new analyses

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:15.810
that I could do.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.640
It's still in SQL, so
there's no learning curve

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.800
for an old analyst
on the old model.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.320
They could jump
right into the tool

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.100
and get started immediately.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.520
And again, more
importantly, back

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.110
to the piece of the
maintenance piece,

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:32.041
there's just no more
infrastructure to manage.

00:09:32.041 --> 00:09:33.540
We were spending
all these engineers

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.170
maintaining this stuff.

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:37.010
Now, they were
essentially freed up

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:40.580
with-- if we decide to go with
BigQuery, they were freed up

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.040
and we could use them on
more important projects.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.920
So we gave it a try and
here's an example of kind

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.240
of how it was implemented.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:53.970
From an access
perspective, it's also

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.370
really easy-- it was
really easy for us

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.240
to just get started
and using-- just

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.450
get started using the tool.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:05.570
So we use permissions and access
all done through Google Groups.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:06.570
Really simple.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.400
And kind of the workflow now as
you enter-- you could imagine

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.320
if you're starting as an
analyst, you enter this group

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.500
and you get this one project
for free called Data Commons.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:22.150
So right now, we basically
just have this big project

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:23.800
that has a bunch of
our core data sets

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:27.400
that have-- these data
sets are owned by teams.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:28.400
You know what's in them.

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.119
You can trust the data.

00:10:30.119 --> 00:10:32.285
They're like very core to
doing any kind of analysis

00:10:32.285 --> 00:10:33.470
at Spotify.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.640
So you get that for free.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.370
And then we're also
organizing projects

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.740
according to specific teams.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.510
So you could imagine
a team that's

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:45.890
working on a running
feature could have

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:48.870
their own project called
Running that gives them,

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:52.270
in addition to their own
sort of little sandbox

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:55.670
that would allow them to upload
whatever ad hoc data sets they

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.240
want to look at just
for analysis purposes,

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.280
ad hoc debugging purposes.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:02.580
They could have the
freedom to do that

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.760
without mucking up and ruining
the experience of Data Commons

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.000
for the larger analysis
at Spotify community.

00:11:14.184 --> 00:11:15.850
And as far as actually
getting the data,

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.050
this is kind of
what it looks like.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.870
We still have a lot of
our jobs writing to HDFS.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:25.400
So pretty regularly,
we have a pipeline

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:29.290
that's pushing stuff
from HDFS into GCS.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:31.390
From there, that's where
BigQuery is calling from.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:35.097
And most use cases, we just use
the out-of-the-box web UI that

00:11:35.097 --> 00:11:35.930
comes with BigQuery.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.790
That's like really
easy to use, really

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.100
easy to get started with.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.800
That probably solves 90% of
the use cases of our analysts

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:47.090
who just want to write a
SQL query, hit a button,

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:48.470
and get results back.

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:49.540
Really easy to do that.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.280
However, there's
also this use case.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:58.280
We use Jupiter a lot within
the analytics community.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:00.250
If you've ever used
IPython Notebook,

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.170
it's basically the
iteration of that.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.540
A nice, little place
that allows you

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.960
to share work and marry your
code with actual result sets.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:10.430
So yes.

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:14.720
So we actually wrote a
few plugins within Jupiter

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:17.950
that would allow us to directly
query from our BigQuery data

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:21.450
sets within this environment
that we were used to.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:22.080
Sure.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.380
We could have used a tool
like Google Datalab, which

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.850
we saw earlier, actually
today, as like an alternative

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:33.270
to a notebooking environment
as an alternate to Jupiter.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.030
But at the time, we
were already so used

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:39.800
to-- it was nice that we didn't
have to sacrifice our existing

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.682
environment and our existing
familiarity with Jupiter.

00:12:43.682 --> 00:12:45.140
And we could very
easily, actually,

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.420
like flexibly still
adapt that environment

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:49.830
into this new data model.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.761
So yes, Datalab
would have been--

00:12:51.761 --> 00:12:53.260
I think could be a
solution as well.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:55.265
But at the time, we
were using Jupiter.

00:12:55.265 --> 00:12:56.640
We decided to go
down this route.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:57.730
And it was OK as well.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.746
So now the data is all
there and it's implemented,

00:13:05.746 --> 00:13:07.995
but then the next piece was
how are you actually going

00:13:07.995 --> 00:13:13.090
to get people at Spotify
to actually go out and use

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:15.896
all this work that
has been already done?

00:13:15.896 --> 00:13:17.770
So from this business
onboarding perspective,

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:20.540
the way we solved
this problem, was just

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:24.500
a lot of workshops, in-person
workshops on BigQuery.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:29.370
So they started with whole-day
on-sites with Google New York.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.120
We had some of the BigQuery
team come to our office

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:37.730
and sit with this early pilot
group of 10 or so analysts,

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:39.500
go over sort of the
guts of how it worked.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.107
They were even nice
enough to sit down with us

00:13:42.107 --> 00:13:43.690
and actually debug
some of the queries

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.160
and answer very specific
questions that we were having.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.600
After that, a lot of us
in this early test group

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:54.510
went out to the
rest of the company

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:56.336
and the rest of the
analysis community

00:13:56.336 --> 00:13:57.710
and shared a lot
of our learnings

00:13:57.710 --> 00:14:01.900
directly in sort of informal
workshops with coworkers.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:04.540
This was, I thought, crucial
to actually getting--

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:07.560
you can imagine, there's a
lot of old-school analysts

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:11.670
who are just used to Hive and
that's just what they know.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:14.470
And they're not going to
really switch their behavior

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:16.660
unless you actually go
out and talk to them

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:18.400
and show them the
upsides, like here is

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.410
an example of what you do now.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.982
Here's why it's
better in BigQuery.

00:14:21.982 --> 00:14:23.690
So those were really
essential to getting

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:27.070
some of the older coworkers, or
more veteran coworkers, moved

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:29.514
over to this new platform.

00:14:29.514 --> 00:14:30.930
And now it's
actually really great

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:32.770
to see that for
all new employees

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:36.150
today, in the onboarding
process we basically

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:37.750
just start with BigQuery.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:40.210
And it's been really easy and
a really smooth onboarding

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:44.230
process, specifically because
of the speed piece of it.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:46.270
Our analysts can really
just get into the tool

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:49.170
and into the web UI
immediately, mess around

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:50.732
with a data set
query, and really get

00:14:50.732 --> 00:14:52.940
a very quick familiarity of
what our data looks like.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:59.130
On a more ongoing basis, we've
done a good job, I think,

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.520
of creating a strong
learning culture at Spotify.

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.570
We have very active
Slack channels.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:07.300
That's typically the first
place for a new big query user

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.230
to go and ask a question.

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:11.840
If that doesn't
work, we typically

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.564
point people to the BigQuery
docs, which are really solid.

00:15:14.564 --> 00:15:15.980
If you haven't
looked at them yet,

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:18.250
like huge improvement
over the Hive

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:21.070
docs if you've ever
looked at them before.

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:25.050
It solves basically most of
our questions that we have.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:26.847
And the Stack Overflow
community also

00:15:26.847 --> 00:15:28.680
has been actually pretty
responsive as well.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.460
So maybe to give it a
little bit more color,

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:39.000
here's some examples of
real-life, you could imagine,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.440
recurring business data requests
that Spotify faces with.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:48.290
And here are some numbers to
show you the 35x and more--

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:50.680
like basically, improvement
in efficiency that BigQuery

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:51.910
has given to us.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:55.505
So I'm just going to
give you an example.

00:15:55.505 --> 00:15:58.290
So you can imagine, like
somebody wants to get KPIs

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:00.600
by specified parameters.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:05.380
Some marketing person wants
to know the MAU of Spotify

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:08.440
in Brazil among 18
to 24-year-olds.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:11.540
So this is an ad hoc query.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.870
It requires an analyst to
write something and pull data.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:16.680
In the Hive scenario,
you'd write this.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.860
You'd maybe leave, go do
something else, come back.

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:22.050
In the new world with
BigQuery, you can write this

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:24.220
and you get results
basically immediately.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:25.190
And it's over.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:28.459
And basically, this is
a huge upside as well.

00:16:28.459 --> 00:16:31.000
Because you could imagine you'd
want to pull KPIs very often.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:33.120
So this could be a very
daily recurring request

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:34.495
that we can do
now in 10 seconds.

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:39.810
Maybe the same marketer wants
to go out and be like, OK.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:42.050
I want to do this
social targeting

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:43.350
campaign in Australia.

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:46.850
Give me all the super-fans
of Foo Fighters or something.

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.510
And I want to give
them an email,

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:49.980
like here's this concert.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.790
And do this targeting thing.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:54.459
I want some emails.

00:16:54.459 --> 00:16:56.750
Well, when they normally ask
analysts for that-- again,

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:58.930
this is very specific.

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:00.910
You'd have to write
some Hive job for it.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.494
It'd take a long time.

00:17:03.494 --> 00:17:05.410
Now that we have all
this speed with BigQuery,

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:08.479
we can do more and more of
these really targeted campaigns.

00:17:11.369 --> 00:17:12.640
Lastly, like top tracks.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:15.089
Top tracks we refer to
internally as kind of our--

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:16.630
it's our word
count, essentially,

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:17.550
problem at Spotify.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:21.410
It's like the bread and
butter analysis question.

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:25.390
So you can imagine a
label person comes to you

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:29.450
and wants to know the top
tracks of Justin Bieber

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:31.400
among 55-year-olds.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:34.490
This is the only job, by the
way, where that's not weird.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:38.087
You pull this all the time and
no one even asks you twice.

00:17:38.087 --> 00:17:39.420
You're just like, that's my job.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.320
I pull Justin Bieber
tracks, I guess.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:46.350
But yeah, like Hive, that
takes essentially a day.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:50.370
And now with BigQuery, you
can deliver on these reports,

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:52.990
again, instantaneously and
just save yourself so much time

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:53.720
going forward.

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:01.240
So these largely, again, are
examples of more recurring,

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.830
like day-to-day
business wins that we've

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:05.677
gotten from BigQuery.

00:18:05.677 --> 00:18:07.260
I'm going to head
it over to Emily now

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:10.225
and she's going to go over
two really powerful examples

00:18:10.225 --> 00:18:13.490
of longer-term projects that
we were really able to leverage

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.560
the platform of BigQuery and
get really great efficiency

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:17.170
upsides.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:18.750
So take it, Emily.

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:22.566
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:26.980
EMILY SAMUELS: Thanks, Kenny.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:30.230
Because of the ease
of use and the speed

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:32.150
that we've gained
with BigQuery, we

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:36.280
were able to solve two problems
that we were facing at Spotify.

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.400
One is around what we
call Audience Insights.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.530
And this is looking at
how users are actually

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:43.680
interacting with our service.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.720
And the other one is around
evaluating A/B tests.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:50.550
But first, I'll go
over Audience Insights.

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:53.690
We have a lot of teams at
our company, like marketing,

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:55.986
and growth, and ad sales.

00:18:55.986 --> 00:18:57.610
And they have a lot
of questions about,

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.770
what our users doing
on our service?

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:02.350
They ask things like,
how many free users

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.865
between the age of 18 and 30
listen to hip hop in the US?

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.000
What platforms do these
hip hop fans like to use?

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:16.720
And how do these platform
habits differ from all free 18

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:20.100
to 30-year-olds in the US?

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.980
Before using Hive
in our old system,

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.610
getting this information and
answering these questions,

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:28.340
it was a really manual process.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:31.340
It involved a lot of time
on the analyst's end,

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:34.544
just pulling the data together
and writing these jobs.

00:19:34.544 --> 00:19:35.960
And then, the
analysts would spend

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:38.660
a lot of time waiting
for Hive to complete.

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:43.230
So it took a lot of time
to answer these questions.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:46.720
The information was
scattered around our system.

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.620
You might need to be an expert
on where to find information

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:51.580
about our users,
where is it stored,

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:54.120
or know somebody
who is an expert.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:59.180
So it's hard just to gather
all the information together.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:02.700
And finally, we had a
lot a Band-Aid solutions.

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:04.660
We had analysts who
would put together

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.670
dashboards with no
clear SLA, and then they

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:09.012
would leave the company.

00:20:09.012 --> 00:20:11.470
So it was unclear whether we
could rely on these dashboards

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:14.630
anymore or how to update them.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.500
We also tried using some
third-party solutions.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:20.390
But this, again, involved a
lot of manual intervention

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:21.890
on the analyst's
part because they'd

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.260
have to gather up all
this data and send it over

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:27.360
to the third party to process.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:29.540
And then, in the
end we'd wind up

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:31.820
having the numbers from
the third-party dashboards

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:33.590
and the numbers in
our own dashboards.

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:35.190
They weren't in agreement.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:38.240
So it was just kind of a mess.

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:40.190
So we decided once
we got BigQuery,

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:43.420
we could build this web app that
we called Audience Insights.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:45.910
And I'll go over how it works.

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:49.290
So it's a web page that
people can go to and choose

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:52.040
a bunch of different parameters,
like whether they want

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:55.120
to look at people in the US
or in other countries, what

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:59.690
ages they want to look at, just
different demographic features.

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:05.300
Once they've chosen all of those
filters, a request gets made.

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:07.430
If we've already answered
that request before,

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.900
it's in our cache and we
return the answer quickly.

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:14.350
So this just increases--
or this makes

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:16.580
the requests a lot faster.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:20.310
But if it's not in our cache,
we have this Python API

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:24.460
that turns that request into
up to 15 different queries that

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.334
run on top of BigQuery.

00:21:27.334 --> 00:21:28.750
Initially, because
we were running

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:34.100
so many queries at once, getting
the results was kind of slow.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:36.610
But we decided to have
dedicated computing slots

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:38.290
to field all of these
queries and that

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:40.975
improved the turnaround time.

00:21:40.975 --> 00:21:42.350
And I'll show you
what this looks

00:21:42.350 --> 00:21:44.920
like with some mocked up data.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:49.460
Here's one graph where you can
see users by age and gender.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:50.960
So you can just see
this break down.

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:55.847
There's also streams by genre.

00:21:55.847 --> 00:21:58.430
So if you want to look at, what
are the top genres that people

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:02.270
are streaming, you can see here
that pop and metal are our most

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:03.510
popular genres on Spotify.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:10.240
And finally, if you want to dig
even deeper, you can look at,

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:13.320
how are people actually
streaming things in terms of,

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:15.340
are they streaming from
their own collection?

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.890
Are they streaming from
their saved playlists?

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:19.320
Are they listening to radio?

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:21.160
Discover weekly?

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:24.170
You can really get a deep dive
into how users are interacting

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:26.720
with our service.

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:29.411
What's great about
this is that now we

00:22:29.411 --> 00:22:31.660
can field all of these
different questions from people

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:33.710
and they feel really
empowered because now they

00:22:33.710 --> 00:22:35.626
can go to this website
and get the information

00:22:35.626 --> 00:22:38.070
themselves rather than
having to go to an analyst

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:40.530
and wait a while and have this
analyst spend a lot of time

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:42.280
answering these questions.

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:45.170
So we freed up a lot of
time for our analysts.

00:22:45.170 --> 00:22:47.691
And we've empowered
different teams at Spotify

00:22:47.691 --> 00:22:48.940
to answer their own questions.

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:56.730
And now I'll talk about
how we use BigQuery

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:59.600
for evaluating A/B tests.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:02.479
I have a screenshot here
of the Spotify Home page.

00:23:02.479 --> 00:23:04.520
You can see there's a
bunch of different shelves,

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:08.130
like recently played
and popular playlists.

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:10.580
But we want to test different
content to our users.

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:12.340
And we want to see,
are they engaged

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:13.820
with this different content?

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:17.010
What if we showed
different genres or charts?

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.050
We want to run a
lot of experiments

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.920
and see our users seeing
a lot of our content.

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:23.400
Are they scrolling down?

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.430
Are they streaming things?

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:30.120
Once they start streaming,
do they stream for a while?

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:32.300
Before getting the answers
to these questions,

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.800
it was a really manual process.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.720
Just like with
Audience Insights,

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:39.940
an analyst would be
involved for every A/B test.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:42.070
They would have to gather
up all of this data,

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:44.340
run these Hive queries.

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:48.190
It was just really involved.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:50.770
But once we got BigQuery, we
decided to leverage its speed

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:54.335
and its ease of use to build a
tool for evaluating A/B tests.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:02.040
We started off using
Scalding to clean up our data

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:05.001
and to join our different
data sets together.

00:24:05.001 --> 00:24:07.500
This was just work that really
couldn't be done in BigQuery,

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:10.439
so we decided to
do it in Scalding.

00:24:10.439 --> 00:24:12.480
And it's something that
you could do in Dataflow,

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:14.146
but our team was
familiar with Scalding,

00:24:14.146 --> 00:24:17.340
so we just kind of
went with that first.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:19.700
Once we had this cleaned
and joined up data,

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:22.240
we put that into BigQuery.

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:25.520
We experimented with a
bunch of different schemas.

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:28.130
But ultimately, we decided that
the rawest form of the data

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:29.770
would be best.

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:32.850
And because of that, now we can
have these scheduled queries

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.750
that run that
aggregate up the data.

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:38.190
And we did this so that
if any of our metrics

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:40.980
change, if we decided we want
to look at something different,

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:43.730
we don't need to actually
change any of the Scalding jobs.

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:48.830
We can just add some
new scheduled queries.

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:51.580
What's also really great about
having our data in BigQuery

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:54.810
is that it's easier
to find data issues.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:56.290
Sometimes, things come up.

00:24:56.290 --> 00:24:59.050
There's a logging issue,
data just looks weird.

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:01.130
And now instead of having
to write Scalding jobs

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:04.769
or look in data in HDFS, we can
really quickly run some queries

00:25:04.769 --> 00:25:06.310
and see where things
are going wrong.

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:08.610
So it's been great for
us for debugging as well.

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:12.920
Now that we had this
data in BigQuery

00:25:12.920 --> 00:25:16.500
and we have our metrics, we used
Tableau to actually visualize

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:19.590
our A/B testing results.

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:23.740
And what's been great about this
is we have these dashboards now

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:27.030
where if you run an A/B test,
we have this visualization

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:29.270
that you just get for free.

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:32.067
And any new test just
automatically flow through here

00:25:32.067 --> 00:25:33.525
because it's
connected to BigQuery.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:39.430
And it's really
empowered our teams

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:42.752
to run their own A/B
tests, and get the results,

00:25:42.752 --> 00:25:44.460
and make decisions
without having to have

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:46.740
an analyst involved every time.

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:48.890
So again, just like
with Audience Insights,

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:51.887
we've really empowered teams
to iterate fast and answer

00:25:51.887 --> 00:25:52.720
their own questions.

00:25:55.339 --> 00:25:56.880
And now I'll switch
over to the demo.

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:03.370
So this is a Tableau dashboard
that one of our analysts

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:07.600
have set up around evaluating
A/B tests for the Home page.

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:10.300
So let's say, for
example, we made

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:13.750
a test around the
recently played strip

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:14.790
that I showed you guys.

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:16.373
And we want to look
at how it's doing.

00:26:18.710 --> 00:26:20.210
You could pick
different tests here.

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:21.490
There's only one for the demo.

00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:23.670
Again, this is all
mocked up data.

00:26:23.670 --> 00:26:25.620
You can choose what dates
you want to look at,

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:28.005
and then whether this is a
test for new users or not.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:34.370
We have a bunch
of different tabs.

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.380
Here is an Overview
tab where you

00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:40.950
can look at how the different
content is doing in your test.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:44.510
We have the percentage of total
impressions at the top here.

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:47.010
So this is just, what are
people actually seeing

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:48.430
on the Home page?

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:51.640
And we have the percentage
of total streams, 30 seconds.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:56.060
So are people actually streaming
things once they see it?

00:26:56.060 --> 00:26:59.700
We can see here for recently
played that for the test,

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:02.560
there's 20% of the
total impressions,

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:06.080
but only 15% of the total
streams, 30 seconds.

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:10.640
And for control, there's around
13% of the total impressions

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:14.470
and 14% of the total
streams, 30 seconds.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:16.190
So just from looking
at this dashboard,

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:17.990
we can see that
something in the test

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:20.920
is causing people not to stream
once they have impressions.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:30.850
We also have this conversion map
where we have different metrics

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:34.770
on the x-axis and the y-axis.

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:36.970
On the x-axis, we have
impression-to-stream

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:38.130
conversions.

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:40.830
So once somebody actually saw
something on the Home page,

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:43.060
did they actually listen to it?

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:46.290
And on the y-axis, we have
streams-to-long session

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:47.490
conversions.

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:49.650
Once somebody actually
streams something,

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:52.179
did they listen for a while?

00:27:52.179 --> 00:27:53.970
And you can see here
the different bubbles.

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:56.854
We have them broken out by
this different content types.

00:27:56.854 --> 00:27:59.270
And any bubbles that are in
the top right, that means they

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:01.910
have the highest engagement.

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:05.010
So we can look here at different
content types, like editorial.

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:09.150
You can see the test is
more engaging than control.

00:28:09.150 --> 00:28:11.900
And for recently played, you can
see that control is a lot more

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:14.050
engaging than the test.

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:18.760
And if you want, you can only
look at certain things in here.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:23.340
You can look by different
types of content.

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:26.370
So for example,
I chose URI type.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:31.137
This is looking at things like,
are users listening to radio?

00:28:31.137 --> 00:28:32.345
Are they listening to charts?

00:28:32.345 --> 00:28:34.270
Are they listening to albums?

00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:36.145
Which types of content
are more engaging?

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:48.710
And here's a deeper dive
into those URI types.

00:28:48.710 --> 00:28:51.050
You can see here we have
three different metrics.

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:54.460
There's impression-to-long
sessions,

00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:57.130
impression-to-stream
conversions,

00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:00.910
and streams-to-long
session conversions.

00:29:00.910 --> 00:29:02.660
You can see here if
you looked at recently

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:06.230
played how the different
URI types are performing.

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:09.670
You can look at radio and
see that for the control,

00:29:09.670 --> 00:29:13.219
radio is much more engaging
on all metrics than the test.

00:29:13.219 --> 00:29:15.260
But for something like
charts, there's really not

00:29:15.260 --> 00:29:16.134
much of a difference.

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:23.790
This is just another way
of slicing up the data.

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.600
We have average streams.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:29.850
And we have it broken out
by different content types.

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:33.440
So we have recently played,
popular, and editorial.

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:35.730
And right now, we're
looking at this broken up

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:37.070
by registration date.

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:38.990
So when users signed
up to our service,

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:42.570
how engaged are they with
this different content?

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:46.900
We can break it up by
gender, by financial product.

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:49.570
So whether people are
free or premium users.

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:54.030
You can see the
breakdown by country.

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:56.620
And also by age group.

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:59.535
So you get a really
good view into how

00:29:59.535 --> 00:30:01.970
your test is performing, and
can really do a deep dive

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:03.620
and slice deeply into this data.

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:10.540
And then finally at
the end, you need

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:12.700
to decide whether you
want to roll out this test

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:13.830
or to roll it back.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:16.519
So we have this
decision-making chart.

00:30:16.519 --> 00:30:18.560
You could see the different
content types and how

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:20.450
they're performing overall.

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:24.120
And these are statistically
significant results.

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:26.780
You could see here
that for editorial,

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:29.304
the control and the
test-- it's hard to tell.

00:30:29.304 --> 00:30:30.720
There's not really
much difference

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.880
of how it's performing.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:34.950
For personalized,
it's pretty obvious

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.860
that the test is performing
better than control.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.260
But for popular and
recently played,

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:43.200
the tests are performing poorly.

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:46.250
And this is for stream-to-long
session conversions.

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:48.090
We can look at
impressions-to-stream

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:52.020
conversions and
see the same thing.

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:55.020
If we want to look
at the test overall,

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:57.352
you can see that the test
is performing a lot worse

00:30:57.352 --> 00:30:58.060
than the control.

00:30:58.060 --> 00:31:01.485
So we probably don't
want to roll this out.

00:31:01.485 --> 00:31:03.110
And now I'll switch
back to the slides.

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:11.850
So again, this has been
a tool that has really

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:14.460
empowered people at our
company to run A/B tests

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:16.170
and really to iterate fast.

00:31:16.170 --> 00:31:19.560
And it's just given them
a way to make decisions

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:21.900
about their own A/B tests
without involving an analyst

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:23.550
for every single one.

00:31:23.550 --> 00:31:25.395
And now I'll turn it
back over to Kenny.

00:31:25.395 --> 00:31:26.145
KENNY NING: Great.

00:31:26.145 --> 00:31:27.404
Thanks, Emily.

00:31:27.404 --> 00:31:31.220
[APPLAUSE]

