Am I on?

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:37.200
Yeah.

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:40.000
So I hope this has been great.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:42.400
This has been
informative in the kinds

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:44.000
of like real-world
business problems

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:46.530
that we've been able
to solve with BigQuery.

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:51.070
To recap, in addition to sort
of the recurring daily data

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:52.670
delivery kind of
stuff that analysts

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:54.470
are responsible
for delivering on,

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:56.050
and in addition
to just improving

00:31:56.050 --> 00:32:00.730
the efficiency of those queries
across the board like Emily

00:32:00.730 --> 00:32:05.247
basically showed us,
the speed with which

00:32:05.247 --> 00:32:06.830
we were able to
operate with BigQuery,

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:08.750
essentially like
fundamentally changed

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:11.860
how the company actually
thought about A/B tests.

00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:13.640
And how they looked
at the results

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:16.640
after launching A/B tests,
which is just huge for us.

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:19.640
And it wouldn't have been
possible without the upsides

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:23.100
that BigQuery was
able to provide.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:28.830
So judging from all this stuff,
we're really excited about it

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:30.730
