00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:32.740
But you know,
BigQuery specifically

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:36.430
is maybe not as mainstream at
Spotify as we'd want it to be.

00:32:36.430 --> 00:32:39.780
So as far as next
steps, we're still

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:43.220
continuing to spread adoption
of BigQuery across the company,

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:46.330
especially as we're
hiring more people.

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:49.420
Initially, as you
saw, for example,

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:53.980
when Emily was talking about A/B
tests, the Scalding piece that

00:32:53.980 --> 00:32:56.350
had to occur before we
actually built this Tableau

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:58.910
dashboard, maybe as
a next step we also

00:32:58.910 --> 00:33:02.790
want to investigate other
tools within the GCP suite.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:04.960
Maybe Dataflow is a
better fit in that piece

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:06.670
and can replace Scalding.

00:33:06.670 --> 00:33:12.050
I also talked about earlier how
we actually still use Jupiter

00:33:12.050 --> 00:33:13.950
as our notebook environment.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:17.380
Maybe we want to invest more
time looking into Datalab.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:19.870
And even Data Studio,
which we saw today,

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:23.420
which was really awesome,
as potential next steps

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:26.120
for our environments.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:29.160
Audience Insights,
it's worth mentioning

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:31.920
that it's the very
first app at Spotify

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:34.960
that we built on top
of this new platform.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:36.750
It's been hugely
successful already

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:39.300
and we're going to want to
continue to make more apps,

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:41.300
like Audience Insights.

00:33:41.300 --> 00:33:44.110
Again, just so we can
continue to empower people

00:33:44.110 --> 00:33:46.940
with data who are not
necessarily technically-- who

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:48.070
