00:33:49.720 --> 00:33:51.440
as well to do more
exploratory analyses.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:56.390
Lastly, it's just
worth mentioning

00:33:56.390 --> 00:33:59.980
Spotify is actually--
on the company level,

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:02.760
we're taking on this new
mission called Data-First, which

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:05.150
is becoming a top company bet.

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:07.850
What that means is that
the whole company is caring

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:10.030
about this concept
of defining, what

00:34:10.030 --> 00:34:14.000
does it mean to be a Data-First
company in today's day and age?

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:17.420
What kind of sub-projects can
we do to achieve this mission?

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:19.830
So here's kind of a taste
of what this top company

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:23.280
bet looks like for Spotify and
what our future looks like.

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:25.219
You could imagine we
have a team working

00:34:25.219 --> 00:34:27.920
on defining our key
metrics for the company.

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:29.520
You know, these are
the metrics that

00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:31.639
allow us to assess the
health and allow our board

00:34:31.639 --> 00:34:35.480
and our lead team to assess
the health of our business.

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:38.239
And you know, this team
would theoretically

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:40.900
come up with those metrics
and also standardize

00:34:40.900 --> 00:34:43.570
the way with which
new functional leaders

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:46.690
or new project leaders want
to select baskets of metrics

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:48.150
to evaluate the work.

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:50.889
So we want to go ahead
and standardize and just

00:34:50.889 --> 00:34:53.179
add more rigor to that process.

00:34:53.179 --> 00:34:55.880
We're doing things like
the reexamining how

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:57.800
we organize and store our data.

00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:01.240
As we move into this
new cloud partnership,

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:02.740
maybe it's a really
good time for us

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:06.447
to actually sit down
and actually rethink,

00:35:06.447 --> 00:35:07.780
is our data model still working?

00:35:07.780 --> 00:35:11.430
Does it still make sense today
as it did several years ago

00:35:11.430 --> 00:35:14.260
when we first came up with it?

00:35:14.260 --> 00:35:15.980
We're also doing
things like launching

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:18.070
this program called Data
University internally

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:19.700
at Spotify.

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:22.100
This is a well-thought
out curriculum

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:25.630
that we largely internally
brainstormed and thought up

00:35:25.630 --> 00:35:30.560
end-to-end that's several weeks
long, intended for engineers

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:34.090
who want to get more interested
into the data-- basically,

00:35:34.090 --> 00:35:36.050
get more interested
within big data.

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:39.050
So the goal of this
kind of program

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:42.360
is just to level up the
quality of data engineering

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:46.950
at the company among all of
our engineering community.

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:50.484
And depending on how
successful that goes,

00:35:50.484 --> 00:35:52.650
we might even consider open
sourcing this and really

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:54.733
officially becoming a
thought leader in this area.

00:35:58.670 --> 00:35:59.910
So to just wrap it up.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:03.090
If all of these early successes
and all of these early wins

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:07.160
are any indication
of working with-- are

00:36:07.160 --> 00:36:10.050
an early indication
of working with GCP,

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:13.250
we're just increasingly more
confident and more inspired

00:36:13.250 --> 00:36:16.530
that Spotify is going to
continue to become a top data

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:18.340
company within our industry.

00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:19.590
So thank you again for coming.

